TOTK: VOTEU writes its vote to a uniform predicate, as it writes its ballot to a uniform register. AVote put the vote of
both VOTE and VOTEU into the lane predicate of that number. OoT's eye shaders take `VOTEU.ANY UR63, UP0, P0` of
"the object's light probe is not empty" and copy UP0 into P0 with the next instruction (PLOP3 P0 = UP0); the red
channel of the probe's light is then computed under P0. With the vote in P0 and nothing in UP0, P0 took whatever UP0
held from an earlier, unrelated test: the red factor stayed an unrelated register value (negative here), and the
whites of the hero's eyes came out cyan. A destination of UPT (the culling kernels' votes) is discarded either way.

--- a/src/Ryujinx.Graphics.Shader/Instructions/InstEmitAmpereAlu.cs
+++ b/src/Ryujinx.Graphics.Shader/Instructions/InstEmitAmpereAlu.cs
@@ -1506,7 +1506,9 @@

             if (vote != null)
             {
-                ASetPred(context, x.Bits(81, 84), vote);
+                // VOTEU's predicate is a uniform one (UP), as its register is (UR): the shaders read it back as one
+                // (PLOP3 P0 = UP0 right after a VOTEU.ANY UR63, UP0, P0)
+                ASetPred(context, x.Bits(81, 84), vote, toUniform);
             }

             int rd = ARd(x, toUniform);
